    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study evaluates sign-aware sparse-autoencoder latents, not individual neurons or neuron poles. Its semantic audit does not test whether explanations predict firing on unseen text, and it does not test the other listed claim versions.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports a blinded semantic audit of two-sided SAE latents and sign-conditioned activation interventions. These concern related feature interpretability and causal behavior, but do not measure the claim's neuron-pole shares or test its specified held-out explanation-prediction claims. The paper's other SAE reconstruction and sparsity results do not directly bear on a listed version.",
    "test_reason": "The study proposes and evaluates a sign-aware sparse autoencoder (SA-GSAE) on Pythia-1B and SmolLM3-3B activations. Its semantic audit and causal interventions concern SAE latents, not the model's own neurons or neuron poles. It reports no neuron-pole job shares (V1/V2), no InceptionV1 analysis (V3), no tool/architecture account of neuron interpretability (V4), no neuron-based attribution graphs (V5), and no held-out prediction or simulation scoring of neuron explanations (V6/V7). It neither tests nor relies on the claim, so T6 is correct.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors' main conclusion is that half-width SA-GSAE matches full-width Gated SAE reconstruction while greatly cutting dead latents. On interpretability, they find nameable semantic opposition rare for all methods but single signed latents causally bidirectional, and they scope their interpretability claims to capacity, dead-latent efficiency and the causal characterization.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper studies sign-aware gated sparse autoencoders, semantic audits, and activation interventions; it does not reproduce the neuron-pole audit or explanation-scoring methods named in the claim.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The semantic audit used two judges and sampled contexts; the causal intervention results vary by backbone and hookpoint. The paper reports these settings but does not test the listed claim versions.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "A blinded two-judge audit of 150 both-sign SA-GSAE latents found coherent opposition between a latent's two sides in 1.5% of consensus items, not above random pairings (0%). Unrelated packing was 64%, indistinguishable from random pairings (65%), AbsTopK (59%) and Gated-SAE antipodal pairs (63%). Only 36% of latents had both sides at least partially interpretable to both judges.",
        "proposed_claim": "At 1B–3B scale and L0 ≈ 64, the two sides of signed SAE latents rarely form a judge-nameable semantic opposition; two-sided usage mostly packs unrelated features.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
          "r4",
          "r5",
          "r6",
          "r7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Sign-conditioned edits of single SA-GSAE latents gave positive Sign-Consistency Scores: 0.97/0.92/0.96 on Pythia-1B and 0.80/0.52/0.80 on SmolLM3-3B. Norm-matched random directions gave −0.35 to −0.49, and opposing drives of near-antipodal Gated-SAE pairs gave −0.38 to −0.71.",
        "proposed_claim": "A single signed SAE latent acts as a bidirectional causal dial, whereas a pair of 'opposite' non-negative latents does not.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r8",
          "r9",
          "r10",
